				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sunday-boy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7168827); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7168827&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609445204" alt="Sunday Boy" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7168827)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sunday-boy" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7168827); return false;" >Sunday Boy</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609439685 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">31 Dec 2020 18:34</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13966130/scp-xxxx-my-friend-ed-seeking-greenlight">SCP-XXXX My friend ED (Seeking Greenlight)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858666">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Horror</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> A standard SCP file with a focus on a journal where the afflicted documented their experience with the SCP, contrasted with interview logs and reports from coworkers documenting their perspective on the afflicted's slow decent.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Researcher exposes self to SCP-XXXX, a standard horrific monster. It doesn't seem like the SCP even notices her, until she starts to see it every time something goes wrong.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The central narrative is a documentation of the Doctor's slow decent into 'madness.' As more and more things go wrong in her life, she ends up doing several self destructive things and perpetuating the cycle. All the while she sees this SCP every time something goes wrong. Everything from accidentally telling the person serving lunch to enjoy their food too to a fatal car crash that takes the lives of one of her coworkers.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The main thing that I'm hoping to make different is to cause the reader to question the reality of what this researcher is experiencing. The whole point of the story is to cause the reader to wonder &quot;Is the SCP causing this, or is it just there when it happens? Or is the SCP even there at all?&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I know that the theme of questioning reality due to an SCP isn't exactly a new one, which is why I'm hesitant to even post this. But I have yet to see a concept similar to this so I was hoping that maybe there's still a market for a monster that just fucks with your mind. Or perhaps doesn't. Who really knows.</p>

							</div>

								
					
